a Canadian junior hockey player involved  in a deadly bus crash is making a  triumphant return to the ice  Ryan's pres netsky was paralyzed from  the chest down when his team's bus  crashed in April 16 people died  including many of his teammates but less  than four months later the 19 year old  is ready to compete again Don Dahler  shows us how stress new skis  determination got him back on the ice  Don good morning good morning after  learning he might never walk again  stretch knit ski told his dad he was  determined to continue his athletic  career by playing sledge hockey given  the extent of his injuries few people  could have expected he'd come so far so  soon at a hockey rink outside Calgary  Ryan stressed miski skates effortlessly  onto the ice it was his first day  learning to play sledge hockey a sport  designed for people with physical  disabilities for Ryan this day has been  a dream of his since learning he was  paralyzed from the chest down I just  wanted to be on the ice I knew my legs  aren't gonna work for however long if I  don't heal but just being on the ice it  would be amazing Ryan was a defenseman  playing his first season with the  Humboldt Broncos in Saskatchewan Canada  the team was traveling to a hockey game  in a neighboring town all I remember was  the bus driver screaming and something  pulling in front of us and kind of  blacked out from there and then woke up  against a semi truck looking at the the  mayhem and you know my first instinct  was to get up and help my teammates and  my brothers and I couldn't move did you  know there was something seriously wrong  yeah it was in a lot of pain and the  only thing I could do was scream for  help but it was tough because I had  broken ribs punctured lung but I mean I  did what I could to help a tractor  trailer collided with the team bus on an  isolated stretch of highway 16 of the 29  people on board were killed in the crash  a few days later Ryan's dad told CBS  this morning about the moment his son  found out that he might never walk again  when they told us  Oh Ryan just looked at us and goes dad  Olympic sledge hockey and get us the  gold are you kidding me no I couldn't  fall apart there had to be strong for  him but just that kids amazing but the  road back to the hockey rink has been  difficult Ryan suffered four broken ribs  serious head trauma a lacerated spleen  and two shattered vertebra after a  six-hour surgery in Canada  Ryan was flown to the Shriners Hospital  in Philadelphia which specializes in  spinal cord injuries when he arrived  Ryan spoke to reporters from a stretcher  still unable to move I just keep my  teammates in my heart to know everyday  set new goals and challenges and I just  remind myself that I do it for them  Ryan's rehabilitation began that same  day the doctors first priority was to  help him adjust a life in a wheelchair  then Ryan's athletic skills were put to  an even greater test with an intensive  program of physical rehabilitation which  included time in the water on the  treadmill and even upper body workouts  there's lots of like muscle  strengthening because my left side was  weaker than my right and I did a lot of  weights and training and now it's  starting to get stronger so I can last  longer in my wheelchair less than a  month after not being able to move Ryan  was strong enough to join his teammates  in Las Vegas for a reunion  shortly after he was a guest of the  Philadelphia Flyers at their training  facility now Ryan is back playing the  sport that he loves doing what comes  naturally moving forward in body and  spirit I just want to say thank you and  you know I'm stay strong  you know life life will get you down but  as long as you you know keep that  positive mentality anything I think is  possible in July the driver of the  tractor-trailer that allegedly caused  the crash was charged with 16 counts of  dangerous driving causing death a court  date is scheduled for later this month  John thank 